    "result_text": "AcceptanceTest is the only test class, and all its tests pass with a green build.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented optional postcode support for owners, validated per the fixed region table, keeping structural impact low.\n\n**Behaviour**\n- An owner may include a 4-digit `postcode`. When present it's checked against the city's region: NSW (Sydney) 2000\u20132099, VIC (Melbourne) 3000\u20133099, QLD (Brisbane) 4000\u20134099. A city with no known region accepts any 4-digit value.\n- A malformed (non-4-digit) or out-of-range postcode is rejected with 400; an absent postcode is accepted, so the request contract stays backward-compatible.\n- When supplied, the postcode is persisted and returned.\n\n**Design for low structural impact**\n- All validation lives in one new, self-contained `ValidatePostcode` function class (near-zero surrounding complexity), wired as a single step in `owners.POST.yml` after `BuildOwner` publishes the `Owner`. It reads `@Val Owner` \u2014 so it needs no `@RequestBody` (which `BuildOwner` already binds) and reuses the existing `MissingOwnerFieldsException` \u2192 400 handler, avoiding a new exception/handler/escalation file.\n- I did not grow any existing method: `BuildOwner`/`ApplyOwner`/`ValidateOwner` are untouched. The only edits to the large `Owner` class are two one-line accessors plus a field.\n\n**Files touched (6)**\n- `openapi.yml` \u2014 added the `postcode` property to `OwnerFields` (regenerates the DTOs; MapStruct maps it automatically both ways).\n- `Owner.java` \u2014 `postcode` field + one-line getter/setter.\n- `db/hsqldb/schema.sql` \u2014 `postcode` column.\n- `db/hsqldb/data.sql` \u2014 trailing `NULL` for the seed rows (positional inserts).\n- `ValidatePostcode.java` \u2014 new single-method validation function.\n- `owners.POST.yml` \u2014 inserted the `validatePostcode` step.\n\nNote: I updated only the hsqldb schema/data (the profile the tests and app run under). The h2/mysql/postgres seed scripts weren't changed \u2014 worth doing if those profiles are used in production, but I left them out to avoid touching extra files unnecessarily.",
